<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Camino Real de Tierra Adentro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th–19th century</span> · Also known as Royal Road of the Interior Land</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Camino Real de Tierra Adentro was the Spanish colonial royal road running some 2,500 km north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>-mining towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> to Santa Fe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a>'s colonists travelled it in 1598, and supply caravans, missionaries, soldiers and traders used it for nearly three centuries, binding the northern frontier provinces to the viceregal capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camino-real-de-tierra-adentro.jpg" alt="Camino Real de Tierra Adentro" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. National Park Service · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CaminoRealAdentro.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
